[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mSection 3 — Error handling[0m

  Two invariants, then one choice.

  [1mTracking must never break login.[0m [38;5;153mwithTracking[39m wraps the [38;5;153mtrack()[39m call in its own [38;5;153mtry/catch[39m. If the sink throws, the
  wrapper warns on [38;5;153mconsole[39m and returns the auth result unchanged. A telemetry outage must never become a login outage —
  this is the single most important property of the decorator, and the reason the [38;5;153mtry/catch[39m lives in the wrapper rather
  than inside [38;5;153mtrack()[39m.

  [1mThe wrapper awaits [38;5;153mtrack()[39m before returning.[0m That keeps ordering deterministic and tests trivial, and with a console
  sink it costs nothing. It does mean a future network transport would sit between the user and their login result — so
  when you swap the sink, that's the moment to add a timeout or move to fire-and-forget. I'll note that in the spec at
  the sink boundary; adding the machinery now would be solving a problem the console sink doesn't have.

  [1mThe choice:[0m what happens when [38;5;153mlogin()[39m itself throws. It can't today — the offline stub only returns — but it will the
  moment the real [38;5;153mfetch[39m lands, and the wrapper's behavior is easier to fix now than to retrofit.

